messages:
Maria Chen: heads-up — running into a weird case where our node SDK breaks on Cloudflare Workers because we assume undici is available. anyone dealt with a universal fetch fallback that also keeps types intact?
Samir Patel: yep, ran into this. simplest approach: prefer globalThis.fetch, fall back to an import of undici only in Node. avoid shipping undici in worker bundles.
Owen Reyes: +1. also watch streaming differences: undici.request/dispatcher vs fetch streaming body shapes can differ across runtimes, so normalize the response interface we expose.
Jin Park: Dev notes from yesterday: Cloudflare and fastly workers implement WHATWG fetch but no StreamsPolyfill for some node-specific helpers we use (e.g. node:stream.Readable.from). we had to add small adapters.
Maria Chen: rough sketch I was testing:
```js
// runtime-safe fetch selection (top-level async allowed in ESM builds)
export async function getFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  // if running in Node, dynamically import undici to avoid bundling in worker builds
  if (typeof process !== 'undefined' && process.versions && process.versions.node) {
    try {
      const undici = await import('undici');
      // undici >=5 exports fetch, older versions used request
      return undici.fetch ?? undici.request;
    } catch (e) {
      throw new Error('undici not installed; please add it as a peerDependency for Node environments');
    }
  }
  throw new Error('No fetch implementation available in runtime');
}
```
Irina Novak: nice. for typings, we added this to our d.ts so TS consumers get correct signatures: 
```ts
declare global { function fetch(input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit): Promise<Response> }
export type Fetch = typeof fetch;
```
Samir Patel: small gotcha: when building for CJS consumers you can't use top-level ESM-only features. we published both ESM and CJS entrypoints and used a tiny shim for CJS that delegates to global.fetch or requires undici synchronously.
Owen Reyes: example CJS shim:
```js
// cjs-shim.js
let _f;
try { _f = globalThis.fetch } catch {}
if (!_f) {
  const undici = require('undici');
  _f = undici.fetch || undici.request;
}
module.exports = { fetch: _f };
```
Jin Park: also think about AbortController and signal semantics — some runtimes attach extra properties to the Response/body. we normalize with a small wrapper: if response.body is a Node stream, convert to web ReadableStream before returning to callers.
Maria Chen: question: should we recommend undici as a peerDependency or optionalDependency in the SDK package.json?
Samir Patel: peerDependency + clear error message is best IMO. it forces customers to pick the right version for their Node baseline and avoids multiple copies. docs should show how to npm install for Node-only usage.
Irina Novak: and document targeted Node versions: e.g. node >=18 can rely on builtin fetch in most distros but not all (old alpine builds), so still useful to call out.
DevX Bot: reminder: create a short example repo under /examples/node-fetch-fallback that shows ESM + CJS usage and tests running in Node 18, Node 16 + undici, and a worker runtime (wrangler or miniflare).
Maria Chen: yes please — I'll open the seed PR. also logging tip: when falling back to undici, log a single dev-only warning so self-hosted infra can catch misconfigurations.
Samir Patel: final nit: avoid bundling types for undici unless you declare them as types in peer deps. consumers using TS should get correct inference from our exported Fetch type if we re-export it in types entry.
Owen Reyes: +emoji :thumbsup: this thread should be the basis for a tiny "Fetch compatibility" doc in the SDK guide — decision, code snippets, and recommended package.json entries.
Maria Chen: perfect. I'll draft the doc and link the example. thanks all :rocket:
